  "strategy": "Let me think about this carefully.\n\nThe task requires finding a sequence of non-negative heights that minimizes a specific evaluation function.\n\nThe evaluation function calculates: 2*n*max(convolution of the sequence) / (sum of sequence)^2\n\nKey observations:\n- The convolution is linear but expansive to compute\n- The function shows interesting scaling properties\n- Toolarge sequences would time out or become inefficient\n- Sequences need to balance being short enough to be trainable and long enough to have favorable convolution properties\n\nApproach:\n\nI'll implement a strategy that:\n1. Generates multiple candidates in different styles\n2. Uses the same core optimization method but allows for variations\n3. Stacks candidates from multiple \"modes\" (initializations)\n4. Cycles through optimization approaches with increasing intensity\n5. Maintains a temperature parameter to balance exploration vs exploitation\n6. Ensures proper normalization throughout optimization\n\nThe main idea is to use different initialization strategies (cosine, raised cosine, Gaussian) and optimize them with stochastic gradient methods until stabilization.\n\nThe optimization will be cyclic and self-similar, adapting parameters based on the sequence length and available time.\n\nWe'll use a temperature parameter that decays logarithmically, controlling the magnitude of updates.\n\nEach \"stage\" of optimization will try a different gradient descent variant and adjust based on local exit conditions.\n\nThe algorithm will track the best individual found and merge promising features across candidates.\n\nImplementation details:\n\nFirst, we need several helper functions:\n- `compute_objective(seq)`: calculates the evaluation function\n- `evaluate_normalized(seq)`: ensures proper normalization when minimizing\n- `grad_approx(seq, idx)`: computes gradient approximations with clamping\n- `grad_descent_momentum(...)`: basic momentum-based optimizer\n- `stochastic_tunneling(...)`: noisy sample-based optimizer\n- `conv_contribution(seq, j)`: computes convolution contribution at index j\n\nThe core optimization will be a cycle through different algorithms, each with:\n- Different momentum schedules\n- Different gradient approximations\n- Different exit conditions\n- Limited memory constraints\n\nWe'll also implement fallback mechanisms and restart procedures to avoid local minima.\n\nThe plan is:\n- Generate candidates using different initialization methods\n- Optimize each candidate through multiple stages\n- Track the personal best candidate\n- Return the overall best after stabilizing or exhausting time\n- Ensure all candidates are properly normalized\n- Use a temperature parameter that decays gradually\n- Monitor time usage and terminate gracefully",
